    <Folder>
      <name>01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic (-20000 to -10000 BCE)</name>
      <description>התקופה הפליאוליתית והאפי-פליאוליתית - 2 sites</description>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic (-20000 to -10000 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2.1 (Pages 43, 44)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic):</b></p>
<p>An ancient well, dated to the early 6th millennium BCE (5900 BCE), found submerged 10m below current sea level. It was a cylindrical shaft 1.5m in diameter and 5.7m deep, dug through clay and into kurkar. A second well was found 100m north, dug into clay and lined with stone.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Paleolithic_Epipaleolithic</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Atlit-Yam</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>עתלית ים</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic (-20000 to -10000 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Coastal Plain</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-20000 to -10000 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.2.1</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>43, 44</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>An ancient well, dated to the early 6th millennium BCE (5900 BCE), found submerged 10m below current sea level. It was a cylindrical shaft 1.5m in diameter and 5.7m deep, dug through clay and into kurkar. A second well was found 100m north, dug into clay and lined with stone.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.9315,32.71089,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Khirbet Jarassur (ח&apos;רבת ג&apos;אראסור)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet Jarassur (ח&apos;רבת ג&apos;אראסור)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic (-20000 to -10000 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Syria</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2.2 (Pages 44)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic):</b></p>
<p>A well found at a Halafian culture site (5200 BCE), considered by Campbell to be the oldest well in the world. Most of its upper part was destroyed during the excavation of an irrigation channel.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Paleolithic_Epipaleolithic</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Khirbet Jarassur</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>ח&apos;רבת ג&apos;אראסור</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>01. Paleolithic &amp; Epi-Paleolithic (-20000 to -10000 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Syria</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-20000 to -10000 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.2.2</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>44</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A well found at a Halafian culture site (5200 BCE), considered by Campbell to be the oldest well in the world. Most of its upper part was destroyed during the excavation of an irrigation channel.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>38.95,36.25,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</name>
      <description>התקופה הניאוליתית הקדם-קרמית - 9 sites</description>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi (עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi (עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jordan</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2.3, 2.2 (Pages 45, 46, 48)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A complex water system including artificial channels (two long, two short) leading to &apos;wells&apos; (interpreted as reservoirs/basins) in a shallow depression. This system was designed to collect and store runoff water from a catchment area of about 29 dunams, potentially holding up to 13,900 cubic meters. A settlement located on the eastern outskirts of Amman, Jordan, near a spring.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Pre_Pottery_Neolithic</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Ain Ghazal / Ibn el-Ghazzi</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>עין ע&apos;זאל / אבן אל-ע&apos;אזי</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Jordan</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-8300 to -5500 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.2.3, 2.2</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>45, 46, 48</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A complex water system including artificial channels (two long, two short) leading to &apos;wells&apos; (interpreted as reservoirs/basins) in a shallow depression. This system was designed to collect and store runoff water from a catchment area of about 29 dunams, potentially holding up to 13,900 cubic meters. A settlement located on the eastern outskirts of Amman, Jordan, near a spring.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>36.833,31.883,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Atlit-Yam (עתלית ים)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2 (Pages 45, 48)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A well, approximately 6 meters deep, demonstrating advanced hydrological and engineering knowledge, construction capabilities, and quarrying skills of the inhabitants. Another well at the site was only partially excavated.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Pre_Pottery_Neolithic</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Atlit-Yam</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>עתלית ים</value></Data>
