          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Coastal Plain</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-8300 to -5500 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.2</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>45, 48</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A well, approximately 6 meters deep, demonstrating advanced hydrological and engineering knowledge, construction capabilities, and quarrying skills of the inhabitants. Another well at the site was only partially excavated.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.9315,32.71089,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Beidha (ביידא)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Beidha (ביידא)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic (PPN) (-8300 to -5500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jordan</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.2.6 (Pages 47, 48)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (02. Pre-Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A settlement where travertine deposits near Bir Abu Rujha and along a sandstone cliff indicate the presence of a now-dry spring that supplied water. The site&apos;s location in a valley allowed for rainwater collection and channeling to create moist soil conditions, and water cisterns might also have been used.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Pre_Pottery_Neolithic</styleUrl>
