          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Coastal Plain</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-5500 to -4500 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.3.1.ב</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>49</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Five circular pits were found, one of which was excavated to 0.7m depth, dug into hard clay and lined with kurkar and limestone. It had an outer diameter of 1.5m and an inner diameter of 0.8-0.9m, identified as a well.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.95513,32.76955,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kfar Samir (כפר סמיר)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Kfar Samir (כפר סמיר)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 03. Pottery Neolithic (PN) (-5500 to -4500 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.3.1.א (Pages 49, 51)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (03. Pottery Neolithic):</b></p>
<p>A rectangular pit (1x0.8m) dug to 2m depth, constructed with alternating layers of stone and oak wood, widening at the bottom. It is identified as a well, unique in its construction method, and possibly used for capturing fresh water springs.</p>]]></description>
