      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ovda Valley (בקעת עובדה)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Ovda Valley (בקעת עובדה)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.5.1, 2.5.13 (Pages 55, 66)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (05. Early Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>Four wells were discovered, cut into a water-bearing layer. One well, well-preserved, has a 1.5-1m opening, is bell-shaped, 1.5m deep, and lined with stones. Its dating to the Chalcolithic and Early Bronze Age is based on nearby flint tools. Two methods of water storage were identified: &apos;granot&apos; (runoff collection basins), dams, and depressions in clay, in addition to wells. These systems were associated with Early Bronze Age 2 settlement clusters.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Early_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Ovda Valley</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>בקעת עובדה</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-3300 to -2200 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.5.1, 2.5.13</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>55, 66</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Four wells were discovered, cut into a water-bearing layer. One well, well-preserved, has a 1.5-1m opening, is bell-shaped, 1.5m deep, and lined with stones. Its dating to the Chalcolithic and Early Bronze Age is based on nearby flint tools. Two methods of water storage were identified: &apos;granot&apos; (runoff collection basins), dams, and depressions in clay, in addition to wells. These systems were associated with Early Bronze Age 2 settlement clusters.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.975,29.932,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shabtin (שבתין)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Shabtin (שבתין)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 3: Approximate Village / Spring Area (&lt; 500m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Samaria</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.5.14 (Pages 67, 68)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (05. Early Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>This Early Bronze Age settlement, located near a spring, also featured unplastered cisterns carved into soft chalk bedrock for rainwater collection, a common practice in Western Samaria.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Early_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
