      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Jerusalem (City of David) (ירושלים (עיר דוד))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Jerusalem (City of David) (ירושלים (עיר דוד))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Hills</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.4 (Pages 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 81)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>The Gihon Spring was the primary water source for Jerusalem from the Chalcolithic period, and its abundant flow was seven times greater than the city&apos;s needs during the Middle Bronze Age. Channel I, a plastered channel partly cut into rock and partly built, is considered the oldest water system in the area, possibly dating to the Middle Bronze Age or earlier, used for irrigating agricultural plots in the Kidron Valley. The central water system of Jerusalem, including Warren&apos;s Shaft (tunnel part), the Great Tower, a pool, and Channels II and III, was carved and built during Middle Bronze Age II to provide the city with an internal water source, especially during sieges.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Middle_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Jerusalem (City of David)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>ירושלים (עיר דוד)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Spring Cave Capture</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Judean Hills</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-2200 to -1550 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.6.4</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 81</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>The Gihon Spring was the primary water source for Jerusalem from the Chalcolithic period, and its abundant flow was seven times greater than the city&apos;s needs during the Middle Bronze Age. Channel I, a plastered channel partly cut into rock and partly built, is considered the oldest water system in the area, possibly dating to the Middle Bronze Age or earlier, used for irrigating agricultural plots in the Kidron Valley. The central water system of Jerusalem, including Warren&apos;s Shaft (tunnel part), the Great Tower, a pool, and Channels II and III, was carved and built during Middle Bronze Age II to provide the city with an internal water source, especially during sieges.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.23584,31.77406,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mari (מארי)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Mari (מארי)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Mesopotamia</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.25 (Pages 94)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>Textual evidence from King Yahdun-Lim (18th century BCE) describes the construction of canals to bring water to Mari, potentially representing an early urban aqueduct, though no archaeological evidence has been found.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Middle_Bronze</styleUrl>
