          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Mari</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>מארי</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Aqueduct Channel</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Mesopotamia</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-2200 to -1550 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.6.25</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>94</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Textual evidence from King Yahdun-Lim (18th century BCE) describes the construction of canals to bring water to Mari, potentially representing an early urban aqueduct, though no archaeological evidence has been found.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>40.8897,34.5497,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Qatna (קטנה)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Qatna (קטנה)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Syria</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.5 (Pages 81)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>During Middle Bronze Age II, a spring was enclosed within the walls of the kingdom city of Qatna, while a stream outside the rampart may have filled a moat for defense.</p>]]></description>
