          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Coastal Plain</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-2200 to -1550 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.6.6</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>82, 83</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A water system consisting of an elliptical vertical shaft with steps, cut into the kurkar rock, was constructed during Middle Bronze Age IIA within the fortified city of Tel Gerisa to reach groundwater, likely for use during sieges.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.80648,32.09327,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Gezer (תל גזר)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.7, 2.6.23 (Pages 83, 84, 85, 86, 93, 94)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>An impressive water system was discovered within the fortifications of Tel Gezer, comprising an entrance, a sloping tunnel with steps, a collection basin, and a cave, dating to the Middle Bronze Age. A large, complex stepped water tunnel, 40m long and nearly 40m deep, leading to a natural spring/groundwater source. The author argues for its construction in the Middle Bronze Age. Numerous bottle-shaped, rock-cut, and often plastered cisterns were found, typically 4.9-7m deep, with drainage channels collecting runoff. Specific examples like 23089 and 34046 are dated to the Middle Bronze Age, with some reused into later periods.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Middle_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Gezer</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל גזר</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Shephelah</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-2200 to -1550 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.6.7, 2.6.23</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>83, 84, 85, 86, 93, 94</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>An impressive water system was discovered within the fortifications of Tel Gezer, comprising an entrance, a sloping tunnel with steps, a collection basin, and a cave, dating to the Middle Bronze Age. A large, complex stepped water tunnel, 40m long and nearly 40m deep, leading to a natural spring/groundwater source. The author argues for its construction in the Middle Bronze Age. Numerous bottle-shaped, rock-cut, and often plastered cisterns were found, typically 4.9-7m deep, with drainage channels collecting runoff. Specific examples like 23089 and 34046 are dated to the Middle Bronze Age, with some reused into later periods.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.91857,31.85882,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Haror (תל הרור)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Haror (תל הרור)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 06. Middle Bronze Age (MB) (-2200 to -1550 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Northern Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.6.11 (Pages 88)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (06. Middle Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A 2m diameter, 11.5m deep well, lined with kurkar stones and rock-cut, located within the Middle Bronze Age city, intended to supply water during sieges. Dated by MB II pottery.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Middle_Bronze</styleUrl>
