        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.03584,32.47469,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mycenae (מיקנה (יוון))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Mycenae (מיקנה (יוון))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Greece</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.7.3.b (Pages 99, 100)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (07. Late Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>An underground system with hidden clay pipes transporting water from the Perseia spring (360m away) to an internal reservoir within the city walls, accessed by a stepped passage. Dated to the 13th century BCE. Additionally, two wells were found within the city walls, and two external springs served settlements outside the walls.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Late_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Mycenae</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>מיקנה (יוון)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Ancient City Water System</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Greece</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1550 to -1200 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.7.3.b</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>99, 100</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>An underground system with hidden clay pipes transporting water from the Perseia spring (360m away) to an internal reservoir within the city walls, accessed by a stepped passage. Dated to the 13th century BCE. Additionally, two wells were found within the city walls, and two external springs served settlements outside the walls.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>22.7567,37.7307,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Ashdod (תל אשדוד)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Ashdod (תל אשדוד)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.7.8 (Pages 103, 104)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (07. Late Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A plastered pool (4162) measuring 3.2x1.5m, found in Area G within a governor&apos;s house or palace (Strata XIV-XVII, Late Bronze Age II). It had two phases, with a brick layer beneath the plaster floor, and a drainage channel leading to a stone-lined drainage pit. Water likely collected from roofs or was manually brought from a well.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Late_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Ashdod</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל אשדוד</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Coastal Plain</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1550 to -1200 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.7.8</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>103, 104</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A plastered pool (4162) measuring 3.2x1.5m, found in Area G within a governor&apos;s house or palace (Strata XIV-XVII, Late Bronze Age II). It had two phases, with a brick layer beneath the plaster floor, and a drainage channel leading to a stone-lined drainage pit. Water likely collected from roofs or was manually brought from a well.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.659,31.75693,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Beit Shemesh (תל בית שמש)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Beit Shemesh (תל בית שמש)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.7.7 (Pages 103)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (07. Late Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>Numerous rock-cut, plastered cisterns (at least 24) were found, dating to Stratum IV (Late Bronze Age). They had narrow necks built up with fieldstones and a sump at the bottom. The widespread use of plaster is highlighted.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Late_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
