      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Upper Galilee</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.7.5 (Pages 101, 102)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (07. Late Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A large underground water reservoir (No. 357) in the Upper City, within the Late Bronze Age palace area (Stratum XIV). It features a built entrance corridor with a corbelled arch and a rock-cut section extending 30m to a depth of 8.5m, ending in a cruciform shape. It was plastered and had carved steps, with a capacity of about 150 cubic meters. Initially, it might have been a burial cave (Stratum XV) before being converted to a reservoir. A bottle-shaped cistern, dated to the Late Bronze Age, found in the western part of the Iron Age water system entrance. Its neck was destroyed during the Iron Age construction, but it contained many intact Late Bronze Age vessels.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Late_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Hazor</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל חצור</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Upper Galilee</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1550 to -1200 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.7.5</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>101, 102</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A large underground water reservoir (No. 357) in the Upper City, within the Late Bronze Age palace area (Stratum XIV). It features a built entrance corridor with a corbelled arch and a rock-cut section extending 30m to a depth of 8.5m, ending in a cruciform shape. It was plastered and had carved steps, with a capacity of about 150 cubic meters. Initially, it might have been a burial cave (Stratum XV) before being converted to a reservoir. A bottle-shaped cistern, dated to the Late Bronze Age, found in the western part of the Iron Age water system entrance. Its neck was destroyed during the Iron Age construction, but it contained many intact Late Bronze Age vessels.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.56806,33.01764,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Taanach (תל תענך)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Taanach (תל תענך)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jezreel Valley</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.7.6 (Pages 102, 103)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (07. Late Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A square shaft (1.45m side, 7.4m preserved height) initially intended as a stepped well to groundwater, but excavation was halted. The entire system, including the shaft, was plastered to serve as a reservoir. Dated to Late Bronze Age I, possibly after Thutmose III&apos;s campaign (1468 BCE).</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Late_Bronze</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Taanach</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל תענך</value></Data>
