          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Plastered Cistern</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value></value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Cisterns or pools were found at Buqei&apos;a, dated to the Iron Age II period.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.4,31.75,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gibeon (el-Jib) (גבעון (אל-ג&apos;יב))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Gibeon (el-Jib) (גבעון (אל-ג&apos;יב))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Hills</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.5, 2.9.51, Summary (186) (Pages 128, 129, 130, 131, 185, 186, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A system comprising a horizontal tunnel from a spring to a collection chamber, and a 52m long stepped tunnel ascending into the fortified city, dated by the author to the 10th or 9th century BCE (early Iron Age II). A large circular stepped shaft, 11.3m in diameter and 24m deep, cut into rock with a spiral staircase, designed to access groundwater. The author dates its construction to the 7th century BCE (late Iron Age II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with two monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Gibeon, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Gibeon (el-Jib)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>גבעון (אל-ג&apos;יב)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Judean Hills</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.5, 2.9.51, Summary (186)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>128, 129, 130, 131, 185, 186, 187, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A system comprising a horizontal tunnel from a spring to a collection chamber, and a 52m long stepped tunnel ascending into the fortified city, dated by the author to the 10th or 9th century BCE (early Iron Age II). A large circular stepped shaft, 11.3m in diameter and 24m deep, cut into rock with a spiral staircase, designed to access groundwater. The author dates its construction to the 7th century BCE (late Iron Age II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with two monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Gibeon, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.18404,31.8489,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Heshbon (חשבון)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Heshbon (חשבון)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jordan</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.13, Summary (186) (Pages 152, 153, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A large rock-cut water pool, 17x17m and 7m deep, with an estimated capacity of 2000 cubic meters, discovered in stratum 17 (9th-8th centuries BCE). It was fed by runoff channels and possibly supplemented by water from the &apos;Ein Hisban spring. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A reservoir or pool was found at Tel Heshbon, dated to the Iron Age II period.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
