      <Placemark>
        <name>Horbat Uza (חורבת עוזה)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Horbat Uza (חורבת עוזה)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.34 (Pages 168, 169)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A covered drainage channel, constructed with stone slabs and over 20m long, was found in the gate passage of the fortress. It started in the plaza, passed under the threshold, and likely terminated in a rock-cut cistern on the slope. Two depressions outside the southern wall of the fortress are identified as likely blocked cisterns, contributing to the site&apos;s water supply during the late Iron Age II. Three large cisterns are located 300m south of the fortress in a wadi. The middle cistern is T-shaped, partially rock-cut and partially built with later barrel vaults, estimated at 400 cubic meters, and features multiple plaster layers (possibly Iron Age I and Herodian).</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
