or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. An aqueduct system within the city, likely feeding a reservoir or pool, was present in Jerusalem during the Iron Age II. A reservoir or pool, likely fed by an aqueduct or tunnel, was present in Jerusalem during the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.23584,31.77406,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Jit (ג&apos;ית)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Jit (ג&apos;ית)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Samaria</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.38 (Pages 173, 174)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>An ancient, rock-cut pool known as &apos;El-Ein&apos; (the spring), consisting of three parts with a total depth of 13.73m. Its water source is a spring, fed by a 1.5m high tunnel from a hard limestone layer, making it a central water facility.</p>]]></description>
