      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kadesh Barnea (Ein el-Gudeirat) (קדש ברנע (עין אל-קודייראת))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Kadesh Barnea (Ein el-Gudeirat) (קדש ברנע (עין אל-קודייראת))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.18, Summary (186) (Pages 159, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A water system in the Middle Fortress (8th-7th centuries BCE) consisting of a stone-built, plastered reservoir (approx. 140 cubic meters) with 25 steps leading to its bottom, fed by a plastered channel from the main spring, Ein Qudeirat. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Kadesh Barnea during the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Kadesh Barnea (Ein el-Gudeirat)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>קדש ברנע (עין אל-קודייראת)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.18, Summary (186)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>159, 186, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A water system in the Middle Fortress (8th-7th centuries BCE) consisting of a stone-built, plastered reservoir (approx. 140 cubic meters) with 25 steps leading to its bottom, fed by a plastered channel from the main spring, Ein Qudeirat. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Kadesh Barnea during the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
