      <Placemark>
        <name>Khirbet Abu et-Twein (חורבת אבו א-טווין)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet Abu et-Twein (חורבת אבו א-טווין)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.29 (Pages 165, 166, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Cisterns or pools were found at Ibn Tuwayn, dated to the Iron Age II period. A large rock-cut cistern, located 5m outside the SW corner of the fortress, dated to the 9th-7th century BCE. It has a heart-shaped plan, measuring 11.95x7.7m with a current depth of 1.7m (estimated original 4m), and an estimated volume of 250 cubic meters. It features a square opening and two layers of plaster, collecting runoff from the fortress and surrounding areas, possibly supplemented by a nearby spring.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Khirbet Abu et-Twein</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>חורבת אבו א-טווין</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Plastered Cistern</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.29</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>165, 166, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Cisterns or pools were found at Ibn Tuwayn, dated to the Iron Age II period. A large rock-cut cistern, located 5m outside the SW corner of the fortress, dated to the 9th-7th century BCE. It has a heart-shaped plan, measuring 11.95x7.7m with a current depth of 1.7m (estimated original 4m), and an estimated volume of 250 cubic meters. It features a square opening and two layers of plaster, collecting runoff from the fortress and surrounding areas, possibly supplemented by a nearby spring.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.1058,31.6492,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Khirbet Abu Tabaq (Kerem Atrad) (ח&apos;רבת אבו טבק (כרם עטרד))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet Abu Tabaq (Kerem Atrad) (ח&apos;רבת אבו טבק (כרם עטרד))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Spring Cave Capture</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Desert</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.30 (Pages 166, 167)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>The Abu Tabaq Cave, located at the foot of the fortress, served as a reservoir. It is 35m long, 7m wide, and 5m high, with a built entrance and steps leading inside. A rock-cut cistern, 18m from the Abu Tabaq Cave, approximately 6m deep with a 1m diameter opening. It is located beneath the fortress, suggesting a shaft for drawing water from within the fortification.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Khirbet Abu Tabaq (Kerem Atrad)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>ח&apos;רבת אבו טבק (כרם עטרד)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Spring Cave Capture</value></Data>
