      <Placemark>
        <name>Khirbet el-Khuwwakh (Etam) (ח&apos;רבת אל-ח&apos;וואח (עיטם))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet el-Khuwwakh (Etam) (ח&apos;רבת אל-ח&apos;וואח (עיטם))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Reservoir</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Hills</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.11, Summary (186) (Pages 151, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Ayyats during the Iron Age II. A large natural karstic cave, 42m long, 4m wide, and 2m high, with an estimated capacity of 300 cubic meters. It was plastered with two layers of plaster dating to Iron Age II, indicating its use as a water reservoir, with an entrance for drawing water and another for collecting runoff or spring water. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Khirbet el-Khuwwakh (Etam)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>ח&apos;רבת אל-ח&apos;וואח (עיטם)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Judean Hills</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
