          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>151, 186, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Ayyats during the Iron Age II. A large natural karstic cave, 42m long, 4m wide, and 2m high, with an estimated capacity of 300 cubic meters. It was plastered with two layers of plaster dating to Iron Age II, indicating its use as a water reservoir, with an entrance for drawing water and another for collecting runoff or spring water. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.18303,31.6884,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Khirbet el-Mekari (ח&apos;רבת אל-מקארי)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Khirbet el-Mekari (ח&apos;רבת אל-מקארי)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Judean Desert</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.30 (Pages 167)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Two adjacent rock-cut cisterns at Ma&apos;aghir et-Tabaqa, 200m NW of the fortress. They measure 13x8m and 15x8m, with depths of 3-4m, and likely served the Iron Age II settlement.</p>]]></description>
