      <Placemark>
        <name>Samos (Eupalinos Tunnel) (מנהרת אופלינוס (סמוס))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Samos (Eupalinos Tunnel) (מנהרת אופלינוס (סמוס))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Greece</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.48 (Pages 182, 183, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>The Eupalinos Tunnel, built in the 6th century BCE, is a 1040m long tunnel cut through a mountain to supply water from a spring to the city of Samos. It included an initial 890m covered aqueduct section, vertical shafts, and a complex rock-cut tunnel with a deep trench for water pipes, demonstrating advanced engineering. An aqueduct bringing water from a distance outside the city was found at Samos, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Samos (Eupalinos Tunnel)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>מנהרת אופלינוס (סמוס)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Greece</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.48</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>182, 183, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>The Eupalinos Tunnel, built in the 6th century BCE, is a 1040m long tunnel cut through a mountain to supply water from a spring to the city of Samos. It included an initial 890m covered aqueduct section, vertical shafts, and a complex rock-cut tunnel with a deep trench for water pipes, demonstrating advanced engineering. An aqueduct bringing water from a distance outside the city was found at Samos, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>26.93,37.69,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.15, Summary (186) (Pages 155, 156, 157, 158, 186, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A water system for the fortress, including a deep well (25.19m deep, 4.6m diameter) outside the walls, and a covered rock-cut channel (24m long) leading under the city wall to three large plastered cisterns (approx. 250 cubic meters) inside the fortress. The system collected runoff and was supplemented by water from the well. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. Wells were found at Tel Arad, which are dated with high probability to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Tel Arad during the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Arad</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל ערד</value></Data>
