      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Aroer (תל ערוער)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Aroer (תל ערוער)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Ancient City Water System</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.17, Summary (186) (Pages 159, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A walled settlement from the 7th century BCE with a large, unexcavated, bowl-shaped depression (40m diameter, 6m deep) within the walls, hypothesized to be a subterranean water reservoir fed by floodwaters from Nahal Aroer. Additionally, wells outside the settlement were likely used. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A well was found at Tel Aroer, dated to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Tel Aroer during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Aroer</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל ערוער</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Ancient City Water System</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.17, Summary (186)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>159, 186, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A walled settlement from the 7th century BCE with a large, unexcavated, bowl-shaped depression (40m diameter, 6m deep) within the walls, hypothesized to be a subterranean water reservoir fed by floodwaters from Nahal Aroer. Additionally, wells outside the settlement were likely used. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A well was found at Tel Aroer, dated to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Tel Aroer during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.97746,31.15098,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Ashdod (תל אשדוד)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Ashdod (תל אשדוד)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Coastal Plain</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> § (Pages 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Cisterns or pools were found at Ashdod, dated to the Iron Age II period.</p>]]></description>
