      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Upper Galilee</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.1, 2.9.51 (Pages 119, 120, 121, 185, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>A large, complex water system was constructed in the 10th-9th century BCE (Iron Age IIA) in the southwestern corner of the tel. It consists of an entrance, a deep shaft (approx. 30m), and a 25m long tunnel leading to the underground water table, designed to secure water supply during sieges. Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. A well was found at Tel Hazor, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were possibly identified at Tel Hazor during the Iron Age II, though their identification is noted with some uncertainty. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Hazor, dating to the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Hazor</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל חצור</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Upper Galilee</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.1, 2.9.51</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>119, 120, 121, 185, 187, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>A large, complex water system was constructed in the 10th-9th century BCE (Iron Age IIA) in the southwestern corner of the tel. It consists of an entrance, a deep shaft (approx. 30m), and a 25m long tunnel leading to the underground water table, designed to secure water supply during sieges. Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. A well was found at Tel Hazor, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were possibly identified at Tel Hazor during the Iron Age II, though their identification is noted with some uncertainty. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Hazor, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.56806,33.01764,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Ira (תל עירא)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Ira (תל עירא)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.33 (Pages 168)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Six cisterns were found within the city, dating to the 8th-7th century BCE. They feature rock-cut necks and rims protected by four large stone slabs. These cisterns were the sole local water supply for the city.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Ira</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל עירא</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Plastered Cistern</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.33</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>168</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>Six cisterns were found within the city, dating to the 8th-7th century BCE. They feature rock-cut necks and rims protected by four large stone slabs. These cisterns were the sole local water supply for the city.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.98567,31.23213,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Jezreel (תל יזרעאל)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Jezreel (תל יזרעאל)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Plastered Cistern</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jezreel Valley</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.20 (Pages 160, 161, 162, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>The site utilized numerous rock-cut, plastered cisterns (dozens to hundreds, with two dated to Iron Age II having capacities of 36 and 56 cubic meters) for rainwater collection, supplemented by the Ein Jezreel spring (Ein el-Meiyiteh) which was also used during droughts. Cisterns or pools were found at Tel Jezreel, dated to the Iron Age II period.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Jezreel</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל יזרעאל</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Plastered Cistern</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Jezreel Valley</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.20</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>160, 161, 162, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>The site utilized numerous rock-cut, plastered cisterns (dozens to hundreds, with two dated to Iron Age II having capacities of 36 and 56 cubic meters) for rainwater collection, supplemented by the Ein Jezreel spring (Ein el-Meiyiteh) which was also used during droughts. Cisterns or pools were found at Tel Jezreel, dated to the Iron Age II period.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>35.32664,32.55765,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Lachish (תל לכיש)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Lachish (תל לכיש)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Shephelah</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.10, Summary (186) (Pages 146, 186, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>The deep well (44m deep) originally constructed in the Middle Bronze Age continued to serve as a primary water source for the city during Iron II. It is proposed that a runoff water reservoir, similar to those at Beit Shemesh or Beersheba, existed in the northern part of the tel to provide an additional water source for the city during Iron II, though its exact location and completion are debated. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A well was found at Tel Lachish, dated to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir or pool, possibly for water storage, was identified at Tel Lachish during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Lachish</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל לכיש</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Shephelah</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
          <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.10, Summary (186)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>146, 186, 191</value></Data>
          <Data name="Description"><value>The deep well (44m deep) originally constructed in the Middle Bronze Age continued to serve as a primary water source for the city during Iron II. It is proposed that a runoff water reservoir, similar to those at Beit Shemesh or Beersheba, existed in the northern part of the tel to provide an additional water source for the city during Iron II, though its exact location and completion are debated. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. A well was found at Tel Lachish, dated to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir or pool, possibly for water storage, was identified at Tel Lachish during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</value></Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>34.8478,31.56667,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tel Megiddo (תל מגידו)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Megiddo (תל מגידו)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Water Tunnel Shaft</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jezreel Valley</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.3, 2.9.7, 2.9.51, Summary (186) (Pages 121, 122, 123, 124, 125, 126, 127, 143, 185, 186, 187, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>Megiddo features a complex water system with two main phases. The &apos;gallery&apos; (secret passage) providing access to a natural cave and well is dated by Yadin to the 10th century BCE (Iron Age IIA). A later, monumental system, also from Iron Age II, includes a large shaft (36m deep) and a 50m long tunnel, constructed to access the same water source from within the city walls. Megiddo&apos;s impressive water system, consisting of a pit and a tunnel, is attributed by the author and most scholars to Iron Age II. A plastered reservoir with a monumental staircase, located outside the outer gate (Locus 2153), was supplied by the nearby Ein Kubi spring or collected runoff. It is dated to the time of Ahab (Iron II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with three monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Megiddo, dating to the Iron Age II. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Tel Megiddo during the Iron Age II.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Megiddo</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל מגידו</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
