          <Data name="Description"><value>Megiddo features a complex water system with two main phases. The &apos;gallery&apos; (secret passage) providing access to a natural cave and well is dated by Yadin to the 10th century BCE (Iron Age IIA). A later, monumental system, also from Iron Age II, includes a large shaft (36m deep) and a 50m long tunnel, constructed to access the same water source from within the city walls. Megiddo&apos;s impressive water system, consisting of a pit and a tunnel, is attributed by the author and most scholars to Iron Age II. A plastered reservoir with a monumental staircase, located outside the outer gate (Locus 2153), was supplied by the nearby Ein Kubi spring or collected runoff. It is dated to the time of Ahab (Iron II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with three monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Megiddo, dating to the Iron Age II. A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Tel Megiddo during the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
