      <Placemark>
        <name>Toprakkale (Rusahinili) (טופראק קלה (רוסהינילי))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Toprakkale (Rusahinili) (טופראק קלה (רוסהינילי))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Urartu</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.42 (Pages 176, 177, 191)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>An aqueduct bringing water from a distance outside the city was found at Toprakkale, dating to the Iron Age II. Around 700 BCE, King Rusa I or II created artificial Lake Rusa by building dams at Kesis Goli and diverting water from the Aliyani river to supply the new capital Toprakkale (Rusahinili) and its gardens. Lake Sihke was used to regulate the water flow.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
