      <Placemark>
        <name>Tushpa / Menua Canal (Van) (תושפה / אמת מנואה (אוררטו))</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tushpa / Menua Canal (Van) (תושפה / אמת מנואה (אוררטו))</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Urartu</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.9.40 (Pages 175, 176)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (09. Iron Age II):</b></p>
<p>The &apos;Mena&apos;s Canal&apos; (Manua&apos;s Canal), a 56 km long aqueduct built by King Manua (805-785 BCE), supplied water from the Engil river spring to Tushpa, overcoming the non-potable Lake Van water. It included stone dams, channels, and support walls up to 20m high, operating for 2500 years.</p>]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#style_Iron_Age_II</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tushpa / Menua Canal (Van)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תושפה / אמת מנואה (אוררטו)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
          <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Aqueduct Channel</value></Data>
          <Data name="Region"><value>Urartu</value></Data>
          <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 4: International Comparative Site (&lt; 100m - 1km)</value></Data>
